<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Brakna</h1><p class="st-meta">1640 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hassani emirate on the north bank of the Senegal River, a major partner of French traders in the Atlantic gum arabic trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Brakna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> emerged in the 17th century in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hassan">Hassani Arab</a> warrior groups established dominance over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholarly tribes after the Char Bouba war of the 1670s. Its territory lay around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleg">Aleg</a> and along the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The emir ruled a hierarchical society of Hassani warriors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> scholarly tribes, tributaries and enslaved people. Wealth came from livestock and especially from gum arabic, sold to French and other European traders at river trading posts in exchange for cloth, guns and customs payments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> fought frequently with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Emirate of Trarza</a> and with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Imamate of Futa Toro</a> across the river.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Succession disputes weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in the 19th century. In 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xavier-coppolani">Xavier Coppolani's</a> policy of peaceful penetration, made Brakna a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, and it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1640 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.34%;width:5.23%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1640 CE – 1903 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Brakna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1772&amp;focus=emirate-of-brakna" aria-label="Emirate of Brakna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4413 1456 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Brakna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4567 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4733 1712h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4733 1712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mauritania. <a href="/atlas?year=1772&amp;focus=emirate-of-brakna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

